#====================================================================
# Draft: petsc-complex 3.25.4 - an upstream build knob that changes the
#        ABI must become its own Conan package, not an option, and the
#        variant's acceptance test must be able to FAIL on the base
#        variant (otherwise it is a false pass)
#====================================================================

id:                     DRAFT-PETSC-COMPLEX-3254-ABI-VARIANT-SEPARATE-CONAN-PACKAGE
trigger_tool:           conan|configure|make
phase:                  recipe-design|testing
error_signature:        --with-scalar-type=complex|PETSC_USE_COMPLEX|PetscScalar.*_Complex|sizeof\(PetscScalar\) == 2 \* sizeof\(PetscReal\)|scalar-type is not a compile option|two consumers.*cannot coexist

severity:               3
category:               conan-recipe-variant-design
match_keywords:         petsc, petsc-complex, 3.25.4, scalar-type, complex, PetscScalar, PETSC_USE_COMPLEX, PETSC_USE_REAL_DOUBLE, petscconf.h, ABI, variant package, false pass, test_package, conan graph, option vs package, f2cblaslapack

title:                  When an upstream configure knob rewrites the public header and doubles a fundamental type, publish a sibling Conan package instead of an option - and make the variant's test_package assert something the base variant physically cannot produce

root_cause:             PETSc's --with-scalar-type={real,complex} is not a compile switch: it makes PetscScalar C99 double _Complex (2x PetscReal) and regenerates the installed petscconf.h with PETSC_USE_COMPLEX. Under the option approach only one binary per conan ref can exist, so a graph containing one consumer that needs a real PetscScalar and another that needs a complex one is unsatisfiable - the resolver would hand both the same object files. The second, quieter failure is test design: a variant package whose test_package only exercises API that both variants implement (VecCreate/VecNorm/MatMult) passes identically on the base variant, so CI green proves nothing about the variant and a mis-wired recipe that silently built real PETSc would ship.
affected_scope:         Any upstream project whose configure/CMake knob changes the ABI rather than the instruction selection - PETSc scalar-type/precision (--with-precision=single|__float128 has the same shape), HDF5/OpenMPI datatype or ABI options, Eigen/Boost wheel-like "single vs double" builds, protobuf lite vs full. Applies to every repo where packages are indexed by name/version only.
verification_cmd:       conan create archives/p/petsc-complex/3.25.4 -pr:h=ohos-aarch64 -pr:b=ohos-aarch64 --build=missing -nr

rec_priority:           1
rec_approach:           sibling-package-with-variant-distinguishing-test
rec_description:        Publish the variant under its own name (petsc-complex/3.25.4) built from the identical tarball, and keep the scalar-type switch hardcoded in build()'s configure_args rather than exposed as an option. In package_info() repeat the ABI macro (cpp_info.defines = ["PETSC_USE_REAL_DOUBLE", "PETSC_USE_COMPLEX"]) so CMakeDeps/pkg-config output carries the variant identity even for -D-only consumers, and rename the CMake/pkg-config file names (cmake_file_name / cmake_target_name / pkg_config_name = petsc-complex) so a target accidentally built against the wrong variant fails at link time instead of at runtime. Then make test_package discriminating: (a) #ifndef PETSC_USE_COMPLEX / #ifndef PETSC_USE_REAL_DOUBLE #error guards so the base variant cannot even preprocess; (b) ABI assertions sizeof(PetscScalar) == 2*sizeof(PetscReal) and PETSC_VERSION_*; (c) value assertions whose expected numbers differ between variants - PetscAbsScalar(PetscCMPLX(3,4)) == 5 (real build: 3), VecNorm((2+1i)*[1,1,1]) == sqrt(15) (real build: 2*sqrt(3)), |VecDot(x, i*x)| == |x|^2 with a non-zero imaginary part; (d) an end-to-end Krylov solve where the RHS is computed by MatMult(A, x_true) rather than hand-written, asserting solution[0] == 1+1i and ||sol - x_true||_2 < 1e-10 so the imaginary part must survive the whole GMRES iteration. Print TAP-ish "ok N"/"not ok N" plus a final "assertions: N, failures: M" line for CI grep. Same rule for precision variants: a complex+single build needs a third package, never a new option on this one.
rec_command:            conan create archives/p/petsc-complex/3.25.4 -pr:h=ohos-aarch64 -pr:b=ohos-aarch64 --build=missing -nr
rec_auto_fix:           false
